@NODE MAIN "Commodore BASIC V2 - Quick Guide"

@{b}Commodore BASIC V2 - Quick Guide@{ub}
For the C64 (x64) and the VIC-20 (xvic): the same BASIC, but other chips
at other addresses. The memory maps are the C64's, the VIC-20 has its
own page.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  @{" Presentation      " LINK PRESENTATION}  BASIC V2 in a few words
  @{" Instructions      " LINK INSTRUCTIONS}  Every keyword, with examples
  @{" Video memory map  " LINK VIDEO}  Screen, colors, VIC-II registers
  @{" SID memory map    " LINK SID}  Sound registers
  @{" VIC-20 hardware   " LINK VIC20}  Screen, colors, sound, joystick
  @{" Examples          " LINK EXAMPLES}  Six short programs to type

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
@ENDNODE

@NODE PRESENTATION "Presentation"
@{b}Presentation@{ub}

BASIC V2 is the language the C64 starts in: the READY. prompt. A line
typed with a number in front is stored in the program; a line typed
without a number runs at once (direct mode).

  10 PRINT "HELLO"       stores line 10
  RUN                    runs the program
  LIST                   shows it

@{b}Lines@{ub}: numbered 0 to 63999, run in order. Several instructions can
share a line, separated by ":". A line is at most 80 characters on the
screen editor.

@{b}Variables@{ub}: only the first 2 characters of a name count (SCORE and
SC are the same variable). Name endings give the type:
  A      floating point number
  A%     integer, -32768 to 32767
  A$     string, up to 255 characters
Arrays with DIM: DIM A(10), DIM N$(5,5). An array not dimensioned has
11 elements (0 to 10).

@{b}Special variables@{ub}:
  TI     the clock, in 1/60 seconds since power on
  TI$    the clock as "HHMMSS", can be set: TI$="000000"
  ST     the status of the last input/output
  pi     the pi key (shift + up arrow): 3.14159265

@{b}Operators@{ub}: + - * / and ^ (power, the up arrow key), comparisons
= < > <> <= >=, logic AND OR NOT (on 16 bit integers: they are also
bit masks, PEEK(56320) AND 16).

@{b}Keys@{ub}: RUN/STOP stops a program, RUN/STOP + RESTORE resets the
screen. Keywords can be abbreviated: the first letter, then the second
with SHIFT (P shift-O for POKE). ? is PRINT.

There is no instruction for graphics or sound: they are done by
writing to the chip registers with POKE. See the memory maps.
@ENDNODE

@NODE INSTRUCTIONS "Instructions"
@{b}Instructions@{ub}

@{b}ABS(X)@{ub}
  Absolute value of X.  PRINT ABS(-5)  gives 5.

@{b}AND@{ub}
  Logical and, also bit by bit on 16 bit integers.
  IF A>0 AND B>0 THEN ...      X=PEEK(56320) AND 15

@{b}ASC(X$)@{ub}
  PETSCII code of the first character of X$.  PRINT ASC("A")  gives 65.

@{b}ATN(X)@{ub}
  Arc tangent of X, in radians.

@{b}CHR$(X)@{ub}
  The character of PETSCII code X (0-255).  PRINT CHR$(147)  clears the
  screen, CHR$(14) lower case, CHR$(142) upper case.

@{b}CLOSE N@{ub}
  Closes the file opened as N.  CLOSE 1

@{b}CLR@{ub}
  Clears all variables, arrays and the GOSUB/FOR stack. The program
  stays.

@{b}CMD N@{ub}
  Sends the output of PRINT to the open file N instead of the screen
  (a printer...). PRINT#N then CLOSE N ends it.

@{b}CONT@{ub}
  Continues a program stopped by STOP, END or RUN/STOP, if it was not
  changed in between.

@{b}COS(X)@{ub}
  Cosine of X, X in radians.

@{b}DATA@{ub}
  Values read by READ, numbers or strings, separated by commas.
  100 DATA 10,20,"HELLO",3.5

@{b}DEF FN@{ub}
  Defines a function of one numeric variable, used with FN.
  10 DEF FN SQ(X)=X*X   20 PRINT FN SQ(4)    gives 16.

@{b}DIM@{ub}
  Dimensions arrays, numbered from 0.  DIM A(100),B$(10,10)

@{b}END@{ub}
  Ends the program. READY. comes back.

@{b}EXP(X)@{ub}
  e (2.71828...) to the power X.

@{b}FN@{ub}
  Calls a function defined by DEF FN.

@{b}FOR ... TO ... STEP ... / NEXT@{ub}
  Loop. STEP is optional (1), can be negative or decimal.
  FOR I=10 TO 0 STEP -2 : PRINT I : NEXT I
  The loop runs at least once.

@{b}FRE(0)@{ub}
  Free BASIC memory in bytes (negative above 32767: add 65536).

@{b}GET A$@{ub}
  Reads one key without waiting, "" if none.  Wait for a key:
  10 GET K$ : IF K$="" THEN 10

@{b}GET# N, A$@{ub}
  Reads one character from the open file N.

@{b}GOSUB N / RETURN@{ub}
  Calls the subroutine at line N, RETURN goes back after the GOSUB.

@{b}GOTO N@{ub}
  Jumps to line N.  GO TO also works.

@{b}IF ... THEN@{ub}
  Runs the rest of the line only when the condition is true (not 0).
  IF X>10 THEN PRINT "BIG" : X=0      IF A=1 THEN 200  (jumps to 200)
  There is no ELSE: the next line runs either way.

@{b}INPUT@{ub}
  Asks values with a ? prompt, an optional text first.
  INPUT "YOUR NAME";N$      INPUT A,B

@{b}INPUT# N@{ub}
  Reads values from the open file N, as INPUT does.

@{b}INT(X)@{ub}
  Integer part, rounded down.  INT(3.7)=3, INT(-3.7)=-4.

@{b}LEFT$(X$,N)@{ub}
  The N first characters of X$.  LEFT$("COMMODORE",4)  gives "COMM".

@{b}LEN(X$)@{ub}
  Length of X$ in characters.

@{b}LET@{ub}
  Assignment, optional: LET A=5 is the same as A=5.

@{b}LIST@{ub}
  Shows the program. LIST 100-200, LIST -50, LIST 300-.

@{b}LOAD "NAME",8@{ub}
  Loads a BASIC program from disk drive 8. LOAD "NAME",8,1 loads at the
  address saved in the file (machine code). LOAD "*",8 the first one.
  LOAD alone: from tape.

@{b}LOG(X)@{ub}
  Natural logarithm of X.  LOG(X)/LOG(10) is the base 10 one.

@{b}MID$(X$,S,N)@{ub}
  N characters of X$ from position S (from 1). N optional: to the end.
  MID$("COMMODORE",5,3)  gives "ODO".

@{b}NEW@{ub}
  Erases the program and the variables.

@{b}NOT@{ub}
  Logical not: NOT 0 is -1 (true), NOT -1 is 0. Bit by bit: NOT X is
  -(X+1).

@{b}ON X GOTO / ON X GOSUB@{ub}
  Jumps to the Xth line number of the list, goes on if X is out of range.
  ON C GOTO 100,200,300

@{b}OPEN N,D,S,"NAME"@{ub}
  Opens file number N on device D (8 disk, 4 printer, 1 tape), secondary
  address S.  OPEN 1,8,2,"DATA,S,W"  writes a sequential file.
  OPEN 15,8,15,"I" sends a command to the disk drive.

@{b}OR@{ub}
  Logical or, also bit by bit.  POKE 53265,PEEK(53265) OR 32

@{b}PEEK(A)@{ub}
  The byte (0-255) at memory address A (0-65535).

@{b}POKE A,V@{ub}
  Writes the byte V (0-255) at address A. Graphics and sound are done
  this way: POKE 53280,0 makes the border black.

@{b}POS(0)@{ub}
  The column of the cursor (0-79 on a logical line).

@{b}PRINT@{ub}
  Prints values. ; prints next to it, , goes to the next 10 column zone,
  nothing at the end goes to the next line. ? is a short PRINT.
  PRINT "SCORE:";S;     PRINT A,B

@{b}PRINT# N@{ub}
  Prints to the open file N, as PRINT does.

@{b}READ@{ub}
  Reads the next values of the DATA lines into variables.  READ A,B$

@{b}REM@{ub}
  A comment, the rest of the line is ignored.

@{b}RESTORE@{ub}
  The next READ starts again at the first DATA.

@{b}RETURN@{ub}
  Ends a GOSUB subroutine.

@{b}RIGHT$(X$,N)@{ub}
  The N last characters of X$.

@{b}RND(X)@{ub}
  A random number from 0 to under 1. RND(1) the next one, RND(-X) sets
  the sequence (RND(-TI) at the start: different each run).
  A dice: INT(RND(1)*6)+1

@{b}RUN@{ub}
  Clears the variables and runs the program. RUN 100 from line 100.

@{b}SAVE "NAME",8@{ub}
  Saves the program on disk drive 8. SAVE "@0:NAME",8 replaces it.

@{b}SGN(X)@{ub}
  Sign of X: -1, 0 or 1.

@{b}SIN(X)@{ub}
  Sine of X, X in radians.

@{b}SPC(N)@{ub}
  In PRINT: N spaces (moves the cursor right).

@{b}SQR(X)@{ub}
  Square root of X.

@{b}STOP@{ub}
  Stops the program with BREAK IN line. CONT goes on.

@{b}STR$(X)@{ub}
  The number X as a string, with a leading space if positive.

@{b}SYS A@{ub}
  Runs machine code at address A.  SYS 64738 resets the C64.

@{b}TAB(N)@{ub}
  In PRINT: moves the cursor to column N.

@{b}TAN(X)@{ub}
  Tangent of X, X in radians.

@{b}USR(X)@{ub}
  Calls machine code at the address in 785/786, with X, returns a value.

@{b}VAL(X$)@{ub}
  The number written in X$, 0 if none.  VAL("12.5")  gives 12.5.

@{b}VERIFY "NAME",8@{ub}
  Compares the program in memory with the file: ?VERIFY ERROR if
  different.

@{b}WAIT A,M,X@{ub}
  Waits until (PEEK(A) XOR X) AND M is not 0.
  WAIT 198,1  waits for a key in the keyboard buffer.
@ENDNODE

@NODE VIDEO "Video memory map"
@{b}Video memory map (C64)@{ub}

@{b}Screen and colors@{ub}
  Address        Hex          Use
  1024 - 2023    $0400-$07E7  screen: 40x25 screen codes
                              (row Y, column X: 1024+40*Y+X)
  55296 - 56295  $D800-$DBE7  color RAM, one color per character
                              (55296+40*Y+X, 0-15)
  2040 - 2047    $07F8-$07FF  sprite pointers 0-7: data block number,
                              the data is at block*64
  646            $0286        color of the text printed next

  Screen codes (POKE) are not PETSCII codes (PRINT): A=1, B=2... @=0,
  space=32, 81=ball, 160=reversed space (full block).

@{b}VIC-II registers@{ub} (53248 = $D000)
  Address  Hex    Use
  53248    $D000  sprite 0 X  (sprite N: 53248+2*N)
  53249    $D001  sprite 0 Y  (sprite N: 53249+2*N)
  ...      ...    sprites 1-7 X and Y up to 53263 ($D00F)
  53264    $D010  bit N: X of sprite N above 255
  53265    $D011  control 1: bit 4 screen on, bit 5 bitmap mode,
                  bit 3 25 rows, bits 0-2 vertical scroll,
                  bit 7 raster line bit 8
  53266    $D012  raster line (read), raster interrupt line (write)
  53267    $D013  light pen X
  53268    $D014  light pen Y
  53269    $D015  bit N: sprite N on
  53270    $D016  control 2: bit 4 multicolor, bit 3 40 columns,
                  bits 0-2 horizontal scroll
  53271    $D017  bit N: sprite N double height
  53272    $D018  memory: bits 4-7 screen at 1024*n, bits 1-3 charset at
                  2048*n. 21 upper case/graphics, 23 lower/upper case
  53273    $D019  interrupt flags
  53274    $D01A  interrupt enable
  53275    $D01B  bit N: sprite N behind the background
  53276    $D01C  bit N: sprite N multicolor
  53277    $D01D  bit N: sprite N double width
  53278    $D01E  sprite to sprite collisions (read clears)
  53279    $D01F  sprite to background collisions (read clears)
  53280    $D020  border color
  53281    $D021  background color
  53282    $D022  background color 1 (multicolor, extended color)
  53283    $D023  background color 2
  53284    $D024  background color 3
  53285    $D025  sprite multicolor 0
  53286    $D026  sprite multicolor 1
  53287    $D027  sprite 0 color  (sprite N: 53287+N)
  ...      ...    sprites 1-7 colors up to 53294 ($D02E)

@{b}Colors@{ub}
  0 black       4 purple      8 orange      12 grey
  1 white       5 green       9 brown       13 light green
  2 red         6 blue       10 light red   14 light blue
  3 cyan        7 yellow     11 dark grey   15 light grey

@{b}Also useful@{ub}
  56320    $DC00  joystick port 2: bits 0 up, 1 down, 2 left, 3 right,
                  4 fire, 0 when pressed
  56321    $DC01  joystick port 1, same bits
  56576    $DD00  bits 0-1: VIC-II 16 KB bank (3 = 0-16383, default)
  198      $00C6  number of keys in the keyboard buffer
  197      $00C5  key pressed now (64: none)
  650      $028A  key repeat: 128 all keys
@ENDNODE

@NODE SID "SID memory map"
@{b}SID memory map (C64)@{ub} (54272 = $D400)

@{b}Voices@{ub}: 3 voices, voice 1 at 54272, voice 2 at 54279 (+7),
voice 3 at 54286 (+14). Offsets in a voice:

  Voice 1  Offset  Use
  54272    +0      frequency, low byte
  54273    +1      frequency, high byte
  54274    +2      pulse width, low byte
  54275    +3      pulse width, high 4 bits (pulse width 0-4095)
  54276    +4      control: bit 0 gate (1 starts the note, 0 releases
                   it), bit 1 sync, bit 2 ring modulation, bit 3 test,
                   16 triangle, 32 sawtooth, 64 pulse, 128 noise
  54277    +5      attack (high 4 bits) and decay (low 4 bits)
  54278    +6      sustain level (high 4 bits) and release (low 4 bits)

  Voice 2: 54279 - 54285       Voice 3: 54286 - 54292

@{b}Filter and volume@{ub}
  Address  Hex    Use
  54293    $D415  cutoff frequency, low 3 bits
  54294    $D416  cutoff frequency, high 8 bits
  54295    $D417  resonance (high 4 bits), filtered voices: bit 0 voice 1,
                  bit 1 voice 2, bit 2 voice 3, bit 3 external input
  54296    $D418  volume (low 4 bits, 0-15), bit 4 low pass, bit 5 band
                  pass, bit 6 high pass, bit 7 voice 3 off

@{b}Read only@{ub}
  54297    $D419  paddle X
  54298    $D41A  paddle Y
  54299    $D41B  voice 3 waveform output: random numbers with noise
  54300    $D41C  voice 3 envelope output

@{b}Frequency@{ub}: value = Hz / 0.0587 on a PAL C64 (Hz / 0.0610 NTSC).
  POKE high byte INT(V/256), low byte V-256*INT(V/256).
  PAL octave 4:  C 4455  D 5001  E 5613  F 5947  G 6675  A 7493
                 B 8410  C5 8910

@{b}Envelope times@{ub} (attack / decay and release), value 0-15:
  0  2 / 6 ms       4  38 / 114 ms     8 100 ms / 300 ms   12 1 / 3 s
  1  8 / 24 ms      5  56 / 168 ms     9 250 / 750 ms      13 3 / 9 s
  2 16 / 48 ms      6  68 / 204 ms    10 500 ms / 1.5 s    14 5 / 15 s
  3 24 / 72 ms      7  80 / 240 ms    11 800 ms / 2.4 s    15 8 / 24 s

A note: set the volume (54296), the envelope (+5, +6), the frequency
(+0, +1), then the waveform with the gate bit (+4: 17 triangle), and
later the waveform without it (16) to release the note.
@ENDNODE

@NODE VIC20 "VIC-20 hardware"
@{b}VIC-20 hardware addresses@{ub}

The VIC-20 has a 22 columns by 23 rows screen, and one chip, the VIC
(6560 NTSC, 6561 PAL), for the video and the sound.

@{b}Memory, by RAM expansion@{ub}
                    Unexpanded     +3K            +8K or more
  BASIC start       4097  $1001    1025  $0401    4609  $1201
  Screen            7680  $1E00    7680  $1E00    4096  $1000
  Color RAM         38400 $9600    38400 $9600    37888 $9400

  Screen: 22x23 = 506 screen codes, row Y column X at screen+22*Y+X.
  Color RAM: one color per character, at color+22*Y+X.
  648 ($0288) holds the screen address / 256 (30 unexpanded).

@{b}VIC registers@{ub} (36864 = $9000)
  Address  Hex    Use
  36864    $9000  bits 0-6 left edge of the screen (moves it sideways),
                  bit 7 interlace
  36865    $9001  top edge of the screen (moves it up and down)
  36866    $9002  bits 0-6 number of columns (22), bit 7 screen
                  address bit 9
  36867    $9003  bits 1-6 number of rows times 2 (46 for 23), bit 0
                  8x16 characters, bit 7 raster line bit 0
  36868    $9004  raster line, bits 8-1
  36869    $9005  bits 4-7 screen address, bits 0-3 character set
                  address: 240 upper case/graphics, 242 lower/upper case
                  (unexpanded)
  36870    $9006  light pen X
  36871    $9007  light pen Y
  36872    $9008  paddle X
  36873    $9009  paddle Y
  36874    $900A  voice 1 (low): bit 7 on, bits 0-6 pitch
  36875    $900B  voice 2 (middle), one octave higher
  36876    $900C  voice 3 (high), one more octave higher
  36877    $900D  noise: bit 7 on, bits 0-6 pitch
  36878    $900E  bits 0-3 volume (0-15), bits 4-7 auxiliary color
                  (multicolor characters)
  36879    $900F  bits 0-2 border color (0-7), bit 3 normal (1) or
                  reversed (0) screen, bits 4-7 background color (0-15):
                  POKE 36879, background*16 + 8 + border  (27 at power
                  on: white background, cyan border)

@{b}Colors@{ub}
  0 black      4 purple      8 orange          12 light purple
  1 white      5 green       9 light orange    13 light green
  2 red        6 blue       10 pink            14 light blue
  3 cyan       7 yellow     11 light cyan      15 light yellow
  Characters and the border use 0-7 only. In color RAM, adding 8 makes
  the character multicolor (pairs of pixels: background, border, the
  character color, the auxiliary color).

@{b}Sound@{ub}: a voice is on with values 128 to 255, higher values give
higher notes; 0 turns it off. Set the volume first:
  POKE 36878,15 : POKE 36875,200      a note on voice 2
  POKE 36875,0                        silence

@{b}Joystick@{ub}: on the VIA chips, 0 when pressed.
  37137    $9111  bit 2 up, bit 3 down, bit 4 left, bit 5 fire
  37152    $9120  bit 7 right, read only after POKE 37154,127, then
                  POKE 37154,255 again (the keyboard needs it)

  10 P=PEEK(37137) : POKE 37154,127 : R=PEEK(37152) : POKE 37154,255
  20 IF (P AND 4)=0 THEN PRINT "UP"
  30 IF (P AND 8)=0 THEN PRINT "DOWN"
  40 IF (P AND 16)=0 THEN PRINT "LEFT"
  50 IF (R AND 128)=0 THEN PRINT "RIGHT"
  60 IF (P AND 32)=0 THEN PRINT "FIRE"
  70 GOTO 10

@{b}Also the same as on the C64@{ub}
  198      $00C6  number of keys in the keyboard buffer
  197      $00C5  key pressed now (64: none)
  646      $0286  color of the text printed next (0-7)
  650      $028A  key repeat: 128 all keys
  SYS 64802 resets the VIC-20 (SYS 64738 on the C64).
@ENDNODE

@NODE EXAMPLES "Examples"
@{b}Examples@{ub}

Type them after NEW, or load them as .bas texts (BASIC menu, "Load
program as .bas"), then RUN. RUN/STOP stops them.

@{b}1. Rainbow border@{ub}: the 16 colors in the border and background.

  10 FOR C=0 TO 15
  20 POKE 53280,C : POKE 53281,C
  30 FOR D=1 TO 300 : NEXT D
  40 NEXT C
  50 POKE 53280,14 : POKE 53281,6
  60 PRINT "BACK TO THE USUAL COLORS"

@{b}2. Guess the number@{ub}: INPUT, IF, random numbers.

  10 X=RND(-TI)
  20 N=INT(RND(1)*100)+1 : T=0
  30 PRINT CHR$(147);"I THINK OF A NUMBER, 1 TO 100"
  40 INPUT "YOUR GUESS";G
  50 T=T+1
  60 IF G<N THEN PRINT "TOO LOW" : GOTO 40
  70 IF G>N THEN PRINT "TOO HIGH" : GOTO 40
  80 PRINT "FOUND IN";T;"TRIES"

@{b}3. Screen memory@{ub}: a line of colored balls written with POKE.

  10 PRINT CHR$(147)
  20 FOR X=0 TO 39
  30 POKE 1024+12*40+X,81
  40 POKE 55296+12*40+X,X AND 15
  50 NEXT X
  60 GET K$ : IF K$="" THEN 60

@{b}4. A sprite@{ub}: a square sprite moving across the screen.

  10 PRINT CHR$(147)
  20 FOR I=0 TO 62 : POKE 832+I,255 : NEXT I
  30 POKE 2040,13 : REM DATA AT 13*64=832
  40 POKE 53287,7 : REM YELLOW
  50 POKE 53249,140 : REM Y
  60 POKE 53269,1 : REM SPRITE 0 ON
  70 FOR X=24 TO 255
  80 POKE 53248,X
  90 NEXT X
  100 GOTO 70

@{b}5. A scale with the SID@{ub}: voice 1, triangle, PAL frequencies.

  10 S=54272
  20 FOR I=0 TO 24 : POKE S+I,0 : NEXT I
  30 POKE S+24,15 : REM VOLUME
  40 POKE S+5,9 : POKE S+6,0 : REM ENVELOPE
  50 FOR N=1 TO 8
  60 READ H,L
  70 POKE S+1,H : POKE S,L
  80 POKE S+4,17 : REM TRIANGLE, GATE ON
  90 FOR D=1 TO 200 : NEXT D
  100 POKE S+4,16 : REM GATE OFF
  110 NEXT N
  120 DATA 17,103,19,137,21,237,23,59
  130 DATA 26,19,29,69,32,218,34,206

@{b}6. Joystick@{ub}: a ball moved with the joystick in port 2, fire
changes the border color.

  10 PRINT CHR$(147) : X=20 : Y=12
  20 P=1024+40*Y+X : POKE P,81 : POKE P+54272,1
  30 J=PEEK(56320) AND 31
  40 IF J=31 THEN 30
  50 POKE P,32
  60 IF (J AND 1)=0 AND Y>0 THEN Y=Y-1
  70 IF (J AND 2)=0 AND Y<24 THEN Y=Y+1
  80 IF (J AND 4)=0 AND X>0 THEN X=X-1
  90 IF (J AND 8)=0 AND X<39 THEN X=X+1
  100 IF (J AND 16)=0 THEN POKE 53280,(PEEK(53280)+1) AND 15
  110 FOR D=1 TO 30 : NEXT D
  120 GOTO 20

And the famous one line maze:

  10 PRINT CHR$(205.5+RND(1)); : GOTO 10
@ENDNODE
